ECHO(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       ECHO(1)

NAME
    echo — Print text to the terminal, because apparently typing it
          yourself wasn't interactive enough.

SYNOPSIS
    echo <text>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'echo' command prints the supplied text to the terminal.

    All arguments are joined together with a single space and then
    printed as one line.

    It is simple, predictable, and completely uninterested in your
    philosophical opinions about newline handling.

ARGUMENTS
    text
        The text to print.

        Multiple arguments are joined with a single space.

        If no arguments are provided, 'echo' simply prints a blank
        line. Sometimes silence really is golden.

OUTPUT
    The command prints:

        " ".join(args)

    followed by the normal newline produced by 'print()'.

EXAMPLES
    > echo Hello
        Hello

    > echo Hello world
        Hello world

    > echo This is a test
        This is a test

    > echo
        

        Congratulations. You have successfully printed nothing.

FUN FACTS
    - Multiple arguments are separated by exactly one space.
    - The command does not perform variable expansion.
    - It does not interpret shell quoting or escape sequences itself.
    - No arguments produces a blank line.
    - It is probably the most emotionally stable command in the CLI.
    - When debugging, 'echo' is always there for you.

